nits:
  - finding: "every 60 s reconcile reads each open task's comments even when unchanged"
    waived: "a handful of open tasks on a loopback instance; the per-poll comment read is what catches a lost comment delivery, and comments do not bump the task's updated stamp"
  - finding: "per-connection threads are uncapped and a trickling loopback client can hold one for long"
    waived: "only a hostile same-user local process can trickle; deliveries and the liveness check run on their own threads and never wait on it"
  - finding: "the seen map keeps done tasks until a 404 prunes them"
    waived: "one small entry per task the router ever raised or the owner commented on; dropping done tasks would replay their comment history if the owner comments later"
  - finding: "asking() restates the open rule beside vikunja.rs's state() and the done filter"
    waived: "one boolean read of done; a shared helper would wrap a single field access"
  - finding: "const ROUTER duplicates the router username in vikunja.rs USERS"
    waived: "USERS is an id table private to name resolution; the listener needs the name, and both are fixed by vikunja-protocol.md"
  - finding: "load_secret's create-600 handling sits in the listener module"
    waived: "the listener is its only caller; init reads the token and never writes a key file"
  - finding: "a failed save_state after emit leaves seen stale, so the next sync repeats the lines"
    waived: "at-least-once is the chosen direction (emit before save); a state-file write failure is also logged via the error path"
  - finding: "any reconcile error, including a state write or non-JSON reply, is reported as unreachable"
    waived: "the line carries the error text, and every such failure means the poll cannot see Vikunja's truth; the router reads the text"
  - finding: "a startup reconcile failure exits after the webhook was registered"
    waived: "the next start deletes and replaces the hook with the same target_url; deliveries to a dead port are covered by that start's reconcile"
  - finding: "the Watch mutex is held across blocking API calls"
    waived: "verified false-positive as an important: the serialization keeps the read-modify-write of seen and the printed lines ordered; calls are bounded by http.rs's 10 s timeout"
